WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:08.996
Module nine moves from traffic to the infrastructure itself: twins of pavements, bridges and tunnels that help decide when to inspect and repair.

2
00:00:09.896 --> 00:00:16.705
An asset twin combines geometry from surveys and BIM, data from sensors and inspections, and a condition history.

3
00:00:16.705 --> 00:00:22.370
A deterioration model predicts the future condition, and the twin turns that into work orders.

4
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.041
Pavement condition is measured as roughness, cracking and rutting.

5
00:00:27.041 --> 00:00:31.212
Bridges are monitored with strain gauges, accelerometers and inspections.

6
00:00:31.212 --> 00:00:35.498
Drones and computer vision now find cracks faster than walking inspections.

7
00:00:36.398 --> 00:00:41.290
To predict deterioration, network-level twins use statistical and Markov models.

8
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.736
Pavement design uses mechanistic models.

9
00:00:43.736 --> 00:00:50.402
For a critical bridge, a finite-element model can be updated with measured vibrations to track its stiffness.

10
00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:56.220
Without a twin, agencies inspect on fixed cycles and repair when damage is visible.

11
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:03.152
With a twin, inspection is risk-based and repair can be timed at the lowest lifecycle cost, before expensive failure.

12
00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:13.530
The asset management standard ISO 55000 frames all of this: decisions about assets should be linked to value, risk and cost over the whole lifecycle.

13
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.502
The twin provides the evidence.
